    "contentEn": "<p>A private transfer from Seville Airport (SVQ) to Hotel Giralda Center covers around 10 kilometres and takes about 20 minutes by car under normal traffic. Seville Airport — officially Sevilla&ndash;San Pablo Airport — sits roughly 10 kilometres from the city centre, and the route in follows the SE-30 ring road before heading into the streets around Calle Juan de Mata Carriazo, where Hotel Giralda Center sits within easy walking distance of several of Seville's best-known landmarks. Rather than working out a taxi rank or a shuttle connection after a long flight, a private driver meets you at arrivals with your name displayed and takes you directly to the hotel entrance.</p>\n<p>Your driver tracks the flight in real time, so pickup adjusts automatically if you land early or late, with no need to call ahead or worry about a delayed connection. Because central Seville includes stretches of narrow, one-way, and partially pedestrianised streets, a driver who knows the area brings the car as close to the hotel entrance as local access allows, sparing you the trouble of working out restricted routes with luggage in hand after landing. You can check live arrival information on <a href=\"https://www.aena.es\">aena.es</a> before you fly, and arrange the full journey — pickup point, flight number, and price — in advance through <a href=\"/get-a-quote/\">get a quote</a>.</p>\n<p>The same private service runs in reverse for your departure, with a driver collecting you from the hotel at the time you specify and taking you straight back to the terminal. Given how central Hotel Giralda Center is, most of a stay here is likely to be spent on foot, which makes having a reliable, pre-arranged transfer for the airport legs at either end of the trip even more valuable, since it's the one part of the journey that can't simply be walked.</p>\n<h2>About Hotel Giralda Center</h2>\n<p>Hotel Giralda Center sits on Calle Juan de Mata Carriazo, a short street tucked into central Seville that puts guests within about a ten-minute walk of both Plaza de España and the Real Alcázar, and roughly eight minutes on foot from Santa Cruz, the old Jewish quarter of narrow lanes and small plazas that sits at the historic heart of the city. That combination — reasonably close to three of Seville's defining sights without being wedged directly inside the most crowded tourist lanes — gives the hotel a practical, well-connected base for exploring on foot.</p>\n<p>The hotel's name nods directly to its proximity to the Giralda, the Cathedral's famous bell tower and one of the most recognisable silhouettes in Seville, visible from various points around the surrounding streets. Staying within easy walking range of a landmark of that scale means a stay built around exploring the old city rarely requires transport once the airport transfer is done, with the Cathedral, the Alcázar, and Santa Cruz all reachable without needing to plan a taxi or bus journey.</p>\n<p>For a hotel positioned this centrally, the balance between accessibility and quiet matters. Calle Juan de Mata Carriazo itself is a smaller street rather than one of central Seville's busier tourist thoroughfares, giving guests a base that's close to the action without sitting directly on top of the busiest plazas, where noise and foot traffic can run late into the evening during the high season.</p>\n<h2>Why a Private Transfer Suits This Route</h2>\n<p>Because Hotel Giralda Center sits within the network of narrower streets that make up much of central Seville, arriving by private car rather than attempting to navigate on foot with luggage, or hoping a taxi driver knows the exact street, removes a layer of uncertainty right at the start of the trip. A fixed price agreed before travel and a driver who already knows the address means the arrival itself is one less thing to plan once you've landed.</p>\n<h3>Navigating Central Seville's Street Layout</h3>\n<p>Much of central Seville was laid out long before cars existed, and the resulting mix of narrow lanes, one-way systems, and streets closed to general traffic at certain times means getting close to an address like Calle Juan de Mata Carriazo takes local knowledge rather than simply following a map. A driver who regularly works this part of the city knows which approach roads stay open and where it makes sense to drop passengers if a particular stretch is restricted, rather than circling the block looking for an entrance.</p>\n<h3>Arriving During Seville's Festival Calendar</h3>\n<p>Semana Santa in spring and the Feria de Abril roughly two weeks later both bring large crowds and processions through central Seville, and streets around the Cathedral, the Alcázar, and Santa Cruz — all close to Hotel Giralda Center — are among the areas most affected by temporary closures. Travellers arriving during either event should mention their exact dates when booking, since a locally based driver can plan a route around closures in advance rather than discovering them on the way from the airport.</p>\n<h2>Exploring on Foot from Hotel Giralda Center</h2>\n<p>With Plaza de España and the Real Alcázar around ten minutes away and Santa Cruz closer to eight, most sightseeing from this hotel happens on foot rather than by car, which is part of the appeal of staying this centrally. For day trips further afield — Córdoba, Cádiz, or the wider Andalusian countryside — our <a href=\"/hourly-chauffeur/\">hourly chauffeur</a> service extends the same private, fixed-price approach to journeys beyond the city, so a stay built around walking doesn't have to mean giving up private transport entirely.</p>\n<h2>A Practical Base for a Short City Break</h2>\n<p>Hotel Giralda Center suits travellers who want a genuine city-centre stay without paying for an address directly on Seville's busiest plazas, and that positioning makes sense for both short weekend breaks and longer stays built around unhurried sightseeing. Because so much of the old town is walkable from Calle Juan de Mata Carriazo, guests often find they use a car only twice during a trip: once on arrival and once on departure, with everything in between covered on foot.</p>\n<p>That pattern is exactly where a private transfer earns its value. A shared shuttle or an unfamiliar taxi adds friction precisely at the two moments when travellers are least equipped to deal with it — stepping off a long flight with luggage, or trying to reach the airport on time at the end of a trip spent almost entirely exploring central Seville on foot. Booking both legs privately in advance removes that friction from a stay otherwise built around walking and spontaneity.</p>\n<h2>Planning Your Transfer</h2>\n<p>Booking both the arrival and departure legs together in advance means nothing is left to sort out once you land or when it's time to leave for your flight home. See our <a href=\"/seville-airport-transfer/\">Seville airport transfer</a> page for general arrival details, and get in touch through the quote form whenever you're ready to confirm dates and flight numbers.</p>",
